  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1102 PM MDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nHigh pressure over the Gulf drifting west into Mexico and a deep \nlow currently off the Oregon Coast descending south off the West \nCoast will dominate the weather pattern through the rest of the week \nand possibly into early next week. The current southwesterly flow is \npulling Pacific moisture up through Southern California and the \nDesert Southwest into the region that supported weak isolated light \nshowers with a few thunderstorms today, and will again tomorrow, but \ncoverage does look to be a little more isolated. As the low bottoms \nout off the Southern California Coast by Wednesday morning, turning \nto the northeast to track up across the Great Basin and Utah by \nFriday, the High pressure moves in over northeastern Mexico to set \nup a more southerly flow than we saw with the last tropical surge of \nmoisture. This scenario sets the deeper plume of tropical moisture \nfrom the remnants of Hurricane Trudy to plow into the southern \nmountains where current guidance has the bulk of the rain falling, \nand casting a rain shadow to the north in the central and northern \nmountains. This being said, there is still a lot of uncertainty \ncreeping into the forecast as early as Wednesday. The latest \nguidance is sliding the moist plume to the west a bit that would \nallow more moisture to push north into southern Utah and to the east \ninto the central and northern Colorado areas. The Atmospheric River \n(AR) models do support this solution with Integrated Vapor Transport \n(IVT) values up to 500 kg/m/s pushing into the central and northern \nareas. As a result, storm totals have increased to one to two inches \nrain in the central mountains and up to an inch farther north. Mean \nwhile the southern face of the San Juan and Abajo Mountains will see \ntwo to three inches rain with locally higher amounts possible. This \nsystem is a very warm system with snow levels remaining above 12K ft \nthrough Thursday creating a risk for receiving rain on snow through \nthe southern mountains that can lead to high stream flows across the \nsouthern areas of Colorado and Utah. In fact, the River Forecast \nCenter is forecasting some of the rivers in this area to reach flood \nstage with this system. Understand that the forecast remains fluid \nat this time, and key details will shift over the coming days. Stay \ntuned for updates on this. Look for the heaviest rains to fall \nWednesday night into Thursday as cooler air and stronger jet \ndynamics move in aloft supporting stronger thunderstorms with \nperiods of heavy rain. These stronger storms may lead to localized \nflash flooding possible across much of eastern Utah and Western \nColorado. Stay tuned on this too as the forecast develops.  \n\nThe showers and thunderstorms will taper off Friday as drier air \nmoves in from the southwest. As the low passes to the northwest, it \nwill drag a cold front across the northern areas Friday mostly along \nand north of I-70, dropping temperatures from Thursday's highs about \nten degrees. Residual moisture across the region will spawn showers \nmostly over the higher terrain Saturday afternoon and again Sunday \nbefore things are finished.",
